<section class="section" id="recommendations">
  <div class="section-label">Section 06</div>
  <h2 class="section-title">Strategic Recommendations</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">Each recommendation is grounded in a specific data finding. Generic workforce advice has been excluded — every action point traces directly to observed patterns in the dataset.</p>

  <div class="rec-grid">
    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">1</div>
      <div class="rec-title">Immediately Reskill Business Analysts and Data Analysts</div>
      <div class="rec-body">These two roles carry the only 0% strong-survival rates in the dataset, with average risk scores of 0.75 and 0.65 respectively — both projected to cross 0.85 and 0.75 by 2035. Excel and SQL are their primary skills, neither of which appears in a single Class 2 record. Reskilling pathways toward Python, Deep Learning, or Security are empirically validated transitions that shift workers into Class 2 territory.</div>
      <div class="rec-evidence">Evidence: Risk scores rising 0.05 per 5 years; 97% of at-risk (Class 0) records in these two roles; zero Class 2 records for Excel/SQL primary skills.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">2</div>
      <div class="rec-title">Prioritize Hiring in AI Research and ML Engineering</div>
      <div class="rec-body">AI Researchers show 0% High Risk and 88.9% Class 2 survival — the best risk/resilience profile in the dataset. ML Engineers follow at 71.2% Class 2 with only 0.25 average risk. These roles also command median salaries of $42,845 and $39,327 respectively — above the dataset average. Investment in these categories offers the strongest ROI in talent resilience.</div>
      <div class="rec-evidence">Evidence: AI Researcher 0% High Risk, 88.9% Class 2; ML Engineer 71.2% Class 2; combined salary premium 25–36% above dataset median.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">3</div>
      <div class="rec-title">Establish Python and Deep Learning as Core Reskilling Pillars</div>
      <div class="rec-body">Python appears in 1,406 Class 2 records — more than any other skill — while Deep Learning follows with 1,075. These are the empirically dominant skills of future-proof roles. Reskilling programs that transition workers from Excel/SQL/Java to Python or Deep Learning have a data-supported basis for improving survival classification outcomes.</div>
      <div class="rec-evidence">Evidence: Python + Deep Learning account for 48% of all Class 2 records; Excel and SQL account for 0% of Class 2 records.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">4</div>
      <div class="rec-title">Do Not Expect Education Level to Drive Resilience — Focus on Role Migration</div>
      <div class="rec-body">Median salary differences between Bachelor's ($31,569), Master's ($31,434), and PhD ($31,672) are negligible — less than 1%. Education level does not meaningfully differentiate compensation or survival outcomes. Workforce planning resources directed at degree requirements for at-risk roles are unlikely to generate returns; role transition programs will outperform education upgrades as a resilience strategy.</div>
      <div class="rec-evidence">Evidence: &lt;1% salary variation across education levels; survival outcomes driven by job title/skill, not credential.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">5</div>
      <div class="rec-title">Leverage Experience Level for Internal Mobility, Not Risk Reduction</div>
      <div class="rec-body">AI risk scores are essentially flat across Entry (0.396), Mid (0.399), and Senior (0.393) experience tiers. However, Senior workers do command median salaries of $48,318 vs. $22,924 for Entry — a 2.1× premium. This salary gap underscores the value of retaining senior talent through role transitions rather than expecting seniority to provide automation immunity.</div>
      <div class="rec-evidence">Evidence: Risk score range across experience levels: 0.393–0.399; Senior salary 2.1× Entry; retention value highest at senior tiers.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">6</div>
      <div class="rec-title">Proactively Address India Workforce Risk — Both Compensation and Survival</div>
      <div class="rec-body">India-based workers earn a median $12,448 — 3.5× below the US median — and show only 8.8% Class 2 survival rates, the lowest in the dataset. These twin pressures suggest India operations may be disproportionately concentrated in high-risk, low-compensation role categories. Auditing India-based role mix for Business Analyst and Data Analyst concentration, and targeting reskilling there first, would address the highest-density risk exposure.</div>
      <div class="rec-evidence">Evidence: India median salary $12,448 vs. USA $43,422; India Class 2 survival 8.8% vs. USA 58.1%.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout" style="margin-top:12px;">
    <strong>Action Priority Summary</strong>
    Immediate (0–12 months): Launch reskilling for Business/Data Analysts into Python/Deep Learning. Near-term (1–2 years): Accelerate AI Researcher and ML Engineer hiring. Strategic (2–5 years): Restructure India workforce role mix; establish Python/Deep Learning as enterprise-standard upskilling curriculum.
  </div>
</section>
